1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,930
Welcome to The Efficiency Edge.

2
00:00:01,950 --> 00:00:03,005
I'm Phil.

3
00:00:03,088 --> 00:00:06,580
Today, we're decoding the money story behind the headlines.

4
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:09,773
On November seventeenth, Nvidia's board said it would not bring

5
00:00:09,793 --> 00:00:11,335
Jensen Huang back.

6
00:00:11,546 --> 00:00:15,380
Six days later, it voted unanimously to reinstate him.

7
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:17,957
To understand why, you have to go back more than

8
00:00:17,977 --> 00:00:19,415
a hundred years.

9
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:21,860
Let's start with the eleven days that shook the most

10
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:24,038
valuable chip company on Earth.

11
00:00:24,550 --> 00:00:26,899
On November twelfth, Jensen Huang,

12
00:00:26,919 --> 00:00:28,315
the man who built Nvidia,

13
00:00:28,335 --> 00:00:30,098
resigned as CEO.

14
00:00:30,181 --> 00:00:32,483
It followed reports about his private life,

15
00:00:32,503 --> 00:00:35,792
including a relationship that reportedly caught the attention of U.S.

16
00:00:35,812 --> 00:00:37,006
intelligence.

17
00:00:37,309 --> 00:00:39,426
The finance chief, Colette Kress,

18
00:00:39,446 --> 00:00:41,708
stepped in as interim CEO.

19
00:00:42,011 --> 00:00:43,280
Markets panicked.

20
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:46,055
That day the stock fell as much as fourteen percent

21
00:00:46,075 --> 00:00:48,082
before recovering some ground.

22
00:00:48,223 --> 00:00:51,523
The next day, Nvidia closed below four trillion dollars in

23
00:00:51,543 --> 00:00:54,804
value for the first time since July of last year.

24
00:00:54,945 --> 00:00:57,735
On November seventeenth, the board said it did not intend

25
00:00:57,755 --> 00:00:59,192
to bring Huang back.

26
00:00:59,589 --> 00:01:01,723
Huang said he was open to returning.

27
00:01:01,865 --> 00:01:04,847
Then, on November twenty-third, everything flipped.

28
00:01:04,988 --> 00:01:08,585
The board voted unanimously to reinstate him as CEO.

29
00:01:08,796 --> 00:01:10,059
What changed?

30
00:01:10,270 --> 00:01:13,299
President Trump says he spoke to Huang that Sunday night,

31
00:01:13,324 --> 00:01:15,052
and then convinced the board.

32
00:01:15,263 --> 00:01:19,086
Nvidia's board says an independent committee is still investigating,

33
00:01:19,106 --> 00:01:22,511
and members of Congress, including Senator Elizabeth Warren,

34
00:01:22,531 --> 00:01:25,013
say the company still owes them answers.

35
00:01:25,410 --> 00:01:28,014
So why would a president get involved with one company's

36
00:01:28,034 --> 00:01:28,973
boardroom?

37
00:01:29,369 --> 00:01:32,008
Because Nvidia is no longer just a company.

38
00:01:32,028 --> 00:01:34,295
It's worth around four trillion dollars,

39
00:01:34,315 --> 00:01:36,640
more than the yearly output of all but a handful

40
00:01:36,660 --> 00:01:37,773
of countries.

41
00:01:37,914 --> 00:01:39,949
Its chips power the AI boom,

42
00:01:39,969 --> 00:01:42,259
and a growing share of the stock market rides on

43
00:01:42,279 --> 00:01:42,881
it.

44
00:01:43,022 --> 00:01:46,062
When Nvidia shakes, retirement accounts shake.

45
00:01:46,366 --> 00:01:48,593
We've seen this kind of power before.

46
00:01:48,990 --> 00:01:50,711
October, nineteen oh seven.

47
00:01:50,731 --> 00:01:53,590
A failed bet on copper shares sparked a run on

48
00:01:53,610 --> 00:01:55,362
New York's trust companies.

49
00:01:55,573 --> 00:01:57,724
People lined up around the block to pull out their

50
00:01:57,744 --> 00:01:58,520
money.

51
00:01:58,823 --> 00:02:01,503
America had no central bank to stop it.

52
00:02:01,807 --> 00:02:03,976
So the country turned to one man:

53
00:02:04,059 --> 00:02:04,515
J.P.

54
00:02:04,535 --> 00:02:05,439
Morgan.

55
00:02:05,650 --> 00:02:08,196
In early November, he gathered the heads of the trust

56
00:02:08,216 --> 00:02:10,274
companies in his private library,

57
00:02:10,294 --> 00:02:11,859
and the doors were locked.

58
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,534
He kept them there through the night until they agreed

59
00:02:14,554 --> 00:02:16,038
to a rescue fund.

60
00:02:16,342 --> 00:02:17,669
The panic eased.

61
00:02:17,689 --> 00:02:20,595
But Congress was alarmed that the whole system had depended

62
00:02:20,615 --> 00:02:21,936
on one banker.

63
00:02:22,078 --> 00:02:25,303
Six years later, it created the Federal Reserve.

64
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:27,858
Fast forward a hundred years.

65
00:02:28,161 --> 00:02:30,145
September, two thousand eight.

66
00:02:30,356 --> 00:02:33,988
Lehman Brothers collapses, and the financial system freezes.

67
00:02:34,129 --> 00:02:37,905
Within weeks, Washington sets aside seven hundred billion dollars to

68
00:02:37,925 --> 00:02:40,838
rescue banks it decided were too big to fail.

69
00:02:41,049 --> 00:02:43,815
Two years later came the Dodd-Frank reforms,

70
00:02:43,835 --> 00:02:46,120
meant to make sure it never happened again.

71
00:02:46,517 --> 00:02:47,751
Notice the pattern.

72
00:02:47,771 --> 00:02:51,269
First, one institution becomes so central that its failure feels

73
00:02:51,289 --> 00:02:52,889
like everyone's failure.

74
00:02:52,972 --> 00:02:55,536
Then, the normal rules bend to save it.

75
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:58,172
And only afterwards do we write new rules.

76
00:02:58,313 --> 00:03:00,534
In nineteen oh seven, it was a banker.

77
00:03:00,554 --> 00:03:02,705
In two thousand eight, it was the banks.

78
00:03:02,725 --> 00:03:05,445
In twenty twenty-six, it may be a single company,

79
00:03:05,465 --> 00:03:07,263
and the person running it.

80
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:10,004
So the question isn't whether Nvidia is too big to

81
00:03:10,024 --> 00:03:10,835
fail.

82
00:03:11,046 --> 00:03:13,475
The question is whether its leader has become too big

83
00:03:13,495 --> 00:03:14,492
to fire.

84
00:03:14,633 --> 00:03:16,993
And if he has, what new rules will we write

85
00:03:17,013 --> 00:03:17,986
next time?

86
00:03:18,128 --> 00:03:20,023
Tell me what you think in the comments.

87
00:03:20,043 --> 00:03:23,344
And subscribe, because the money story behind AI is only

88
00:03:23,364 --> 00:03:24,976
just getting started.
